VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: P6
ATTEMPT: 1
---
EVIDENCE:

Executed (not just read): `go test ./internal/services/whatifmcp/... -race -count=2 -v` (all 93+ subtests pass, no data races); two throwaway adversarial test files written to the package, run, and deleted before finishing (`git status --porcelain internal/services/whatifmcp/` confirmed clean of my scratch files afterward — repo is otherwise mid-task with P6's own untracked files as expected).

1. Dataloader cost/parity (insights.go:43-67, dataloader/loader.go:138-221, handlers/insights/handlers.go:1043-1108)
   - Cost: Transactions() builds a brand-new *dataloader.DataLoader and re-runs the FULL pipeline (glob, parse, transfer-filter, classify, dedupe, near-dup detection, aliases, major-expense stamping, Amazon enrichment) on every call, no caching in Source. This is NOT a regression introduced here — cmd/server's own HTTP handlers (handleInsights, handleRecurringPartial, etc., handlers.go:1043/1111/1139/1173/1239/1275) do the exact same thing: `loader.LoadData()` fresh on every request, no memoized TransactionSet anywhere in this codebase. Acceptable; consistent with existing architecture. The underlying storage.Storage file-content cache (mtime+size keyed, storage.go:30-39) still avoids re-reading unchanged file bytes from disk across repeated calls.
   - Parity: when settingsDir == cfg.DataDirectory+"/settings" (the default and BUDGET_DATA_DIR-derived paths, cmd/whatif-mcp/main.go:29-37 mirrors config.go:56/77-78 exactly), Transactions() reads the SAME CSV directory through the SAME storage.Storage.OpenFile/ReadFile path as cmd/server's global `loader` (main.go:58 `loader = dataloader.New(cfg.DataDirectory, store)`), running the identical LoadData pipeline. anomalies.Detect and pricecreep.Detect both call ts.Active() internally (anomalies.go:152, confirmed) so Suppressed near-duplicates are excluded the same way regardless of whether the caller pre-filters — no divergence there. Both packages are also independently confirmed to already scope to `TransactionType==Outflow && Amount<0` per the ANALYTICS_PORT_SPEC.md Ruling (anomalies.go:7-11, pricecreep.go:59-64), matching the tool descriptions exactly.
   - One inherent (not introduced by this diff) parity gap: cmd/server's file-manager page lets a user disable individual CSV files in-memory on the shared `loader` singleton (handlers/explorer/handlers.go:435-464, `loader.SetEnabledFiles`) — this is never persisted to disk, so it is invisible to any other process by construction, whatif-mcp included. A user who has toggled a file off on the dashboard will see it excluded there but included in get_anomalies/get_price_creep. This is a structural consequence of running two separate OS processes with no shared state channel, not something Transactions()'s design could fix; noting it as a finding, not a failure.

2. Settings-dir-parent assumption (insights.go:36-42,51 vs cmd/whatif-mcp/main.go:29-37 vs live.go:185-203) — MATERIAL DEFECT, empirically confirmed
   - The default/env paths (no `-data` flag, or `BUDGET_DATA_DIR`) always produce settingsDir = `<dataDir>/settings`, so `filepath.Dir(settingsDir)` correctly recovers dataDir. Fine.
   - But `-data` is a supported, documented flag (`flag.String("data", "", "settings directory (default ./data/settings)")`) that is used AS-IS for settingsDir with no validation that its basename is "settings" — checkSettingsDir (main.go:56-61) only checks readability, not shape.
   - live.go's spawnArgs (used by open_page/apply_changes when spawning cmd/server) explicitly guards this: `if filepath.Base(abs) != "settings" { return "", fmt.Errorf("cannot start a server for %s: BUDGET_DATA_DIR always resolves to <dir>/settings, so no value produces this path...") }` (live.go:197-201) — a clear, actionable error.
   - Transactions() has NO equivalent guard. I constructed the failure case directly: a temp settings dir named `mydata-settings` (parent exists, is readable, but is NOT where the CSVs actually live — they're in a sibling `actual-data/` the tool never looks at). Result: `os.Stat(filepath.Dir(settingsDir))` succeeds (the coincidentally-existing parent), `dataloader.New` globs 0 CSVs there, and `Transactions()` returns `(models.NewTransactionSet(nil), nil)` — **no error, count: 0**, indistinguishable from "genuinely no anomalies here." Verified with an executed test (`TestZZ_CustomDataFlagWrongBasenameSilentlyEmpty`, since removed): `err=<nil> ts.Len()=0`.
   - This means the exact same misconfiguration (a custom `-data` value not literally named "settings") produces a clear startup-time-adjacent error from open_page/apply_changes but a silent, confidently-empty "0 anomalies / 0 price-creep items" from get_anomalies/get_price_creep in the same server process — a materially misleading result a user could reasonably read as "your finances look fine." This is exactly the "silent wrong-dir failure" class the task asked me to check for, and it is real and reachable through a documented flag, not a contrived edge.
   - Existing tests (transactions_test.go) cover only "parent missing" (errors, good) and "parent exists but genuinely empty" (empty, correctly no-error) — neither exercises "parent exists but is the WRONG directory," so this gap has no regression coverage.
   - Suggested fix (not applied — I am read-only): either reuse spawnArgs' `filepath.Base(settingsDir) != "settings"` check in Transactions() (or centralize it), or have cmd/whatif-mcp/main.go pass cfg.DataDirectory through explicitly instead of re-deriving it via filepath.Dir deep inside whatifmcp.

3. Tool contract honesty (server.go:298-341) — accurate.
   - get_anomalies' description states full-history baselines, display-only windowing, outflows-only with signed (negative) amounts, inclusive YYYY-MM-DD, invalid date = tool error — all verified true against anomalyRows/parseWindowDate/inWindow and the planted-anomaly test (Amount == -400, not abs).
   - get_price_creep's description states full-history-only (no window param), outflows-only, absolute (positive) amounts — verified true against pricecreep.Detect's own doc comment and priceCreepRows (round0 applied directly, no further abs needed since pricecreep already returns absolute).
   - pct_change is a percentage number (30.0 meaning 30%), not a fraction — matches the planted-series test (PctChange == 30) and is the conventional reading; low risk of model misinterpretation, not flagging.
   - Minor, non-blocking inconsistency: get_months explicitly validates and errors on an inverted range (`TestMonthWindow_RejectsInvertedRangeStatingValidRange` exists), but get_anomalies has no equivalent start>end check — an inverted window just silently matches nothing via inWindow's two independent bound checks, which is harmless (a true statement — nothing is in an empty window) but is a rigor inconsistency within the same package. Not a failure on its own.

4. Window filtering (insights.go:153-172) — correct. Both bounds inclusive, computed as [00:00:00, 23:59:59.999999999] in the parsed date's own Location. parseWindowDate uses `time.Parse("2006-01-02", ...)`, which defaults to UTC — and dataloader's own parseDate (loader.go:455-474) also defaults to UTC for every format it tries, so transaction dates and window bounds share the same zero-offset location; no tz skew. Confirmed against TestGetAnomaliesTool_WindowFiltersDisplayOnly.

5. Determinism + races — clean. `go test ./internal/services/whatifmcp/... -race -count=2` passes with no race reports. Source.Transactions() does not mutate shared Source state; it allocates a fresh *dataloader.DataLoader per call (own unresolvedDuplicates/resolvedDuplicates fields), so concurrent tool calls never share a DataLoader. The only cross-call shared mutable state is *storage.Storage, which already carries its own sync.RWMutex (mu) and a separate cacheMu for its file-content cache — pre-existing, already exercised concurrently by cmd/server's HTTP handlers. anomalies.Detect/pricecreep.Detect are documented and behave as pure functions of their input (sort tie-break on Hash makes ordering deterministic). txSource is nil in production and never written after NewServer's src.live assignment; only test code (same package) sets it, pre-call.

6. Locked-storage empty-results edge (insights.go:44-49) — characterization VERIFIED ACCURATE, not re-flagging.
   - Without the proactive check, the underlying mechanism is real: dataloader.loadCSVFile's per-file errors (storage.go:243-246 `"file is encrypted but storage is locked"`) are caught and logged-and-skipped inside LoadDataContext's file loop (loader.go:179-183, `continue` on error), so an entirely-locked store would silently yield `len(allTransactions)==0` → `models.NewTransactionSet(nil), nil` with NO error — exactly the "empty-results" characterization.
   - The worker's guard (`s.store.IsEncrypted() && !s.store.IsUnlocked()`) correctly intercepts this before ever calling dataloader, returning a clear, actionable error. I verified this directly with an executed test (`TestZZ_LockedStorageErrorsClearlyNotSilentlyEmpty`, since removed): a settings dir marked `.encrypted` and never unlocked produced `err = "cannot load transaction history: storage is encrypted and locked; unlock it via the budget2 web UI (/unlock) first"`, not an empty silent result.
   - Residual risk (why it's KNOWN/punch-listed rather than fully closed): the check-then-load is not atomic — a concurrent `/lock` between the IsEncrypted/IsUnlocked check and the LoadData call could still hit the silent-empty path underneath. This requires a racing concurrent lock action during a single tool call; narrow, and consistent with how the rest of this codebase treats storage lock state (no locking primitive elsewhere spans a full read+process operation either). Accurate characterization; agree it should stay punch-listed, not block P6.

MATERIALITY JUDGMENT: Item 2 is the disqualifying finding. It is not hypothetical — I reproduced it — and it is reachable via a supported, documented flag (`-data`), producing a confidently-wrong "no anomalies/no price-creep" answer with zero error signal, while a sibling code path in the same PR-adjacent file (live.go's spawnArgs) already demonstrates the team knows how to guard exactly this misconfiguration. This is the "silent wrong-dir failure" class named as PASS-blocking. Everything else (parity, contract honesty, window semantics, races, the locked-storage characterization) checks out clean.
